With a sample size of 15, no more than 7 variables should be included. 

July would likely be best, since this month represents the extreme end of temperature values generally. This also includes complete data between the ponds, since most of the dry-out ponds dried by late July or August. 

Best candidates:

canopy_50 
imp_100
july_water_mean OR july_water-air OR av_hrs_water_july_mintomax
july_HSF
veg_mean_cover

pH does not technically relate to drying mechanics, so opting to ignore it. 

canopy → structural shading potential
impervious → runoff / heat loading
water-air → thermal buffering
warming time → thermal inertia
HSF → realized shade persistence
vegetation → biological response (increases vegetation, thereby using more water resources)


But let's do correlation checks beforehand to test variables anc check for redundancy. 
Then we need to standardize everything using z scores. 

If PC1 loads on multiple temperature variables, the variables selection failed. 

