vars

```

```{r}
cor_mat <- cor(vars, use = "pairwise.complete.obs")
round(cor_mat, 2)
```

```{r}

high_cor_idx <- which(abs(cor_mat) > 0.7 & abs(cor_mat) < 1, arr.ind = TRUE)

high_cor_df <- data.frame(
  var1 = rownames(cor_mat)[high_cor_idx[, 1]],
  var2 = colnames(cor_mat)[high_cor_idx[, 2]],
  corr = cor_mat[high_cor_idx]
)


```

```{r}
high_cor_df <- high_cor_df[high_cor_df$var1 != high_cor_df$var2, ]

high_cor_df <- high_cor_df[
  !duplicated(t(apply(high_cor_df[, 1:2], 1, sort))),
]

high_cor_df <- high_cor_df[order(-abs(high_cor_df$corr)), ]

high_cor_df
```


looking for:

|r| ≥ 0.7 → moderate redundancy
|r| ≥ 0.8 → strong redundancy (remove one)

Results:
july_hsf vs july_water_air = -0.71

This is not just “high correlation.” It’s telling you:

high persistent shade → cooler water relative to air
low persistent shade → hotter water relative to air

So:

👉 july_hsf = driver (cause)
👉 july_water_air = response (effect)

If you include both, PCA will double-count the same mechanism and muddy interpretation.


