Biplot: what PC1 actually represents

Your system is NOT driven by one gradient.

It’s driven by:

1. Thermal buffering (PC1)
2. Vegetation structure vs growth dynamics (PC2)
3. Landscape context (PC3)


Look at the loadings:

Strong contributors to PC1:
av_hrs_water_july_mintomax → -0.57
july_sei → -0.59
slope_per_day_log → -0.32
mean_season_ph → +0.39
Interpretation

PC1 is NOT a simple “shade” axis.

It is:

👉 Thermal buffering vs exposure

Negative PC1 (left):

high shade duration
high shade evenness
longer time to max temperature
lower pH
→ cool, buffered, stable systems

Positive PC1 (right):

lower shade influence
higher pH
→ more exposed, thermally active systems

PC2: this is your vegetation axis
Strong contributors:
veg_mean_cover_july → +0.55
imp_100 → +0.37
canopy_50 → +0.35
slope_per_day_log → -0.48
Interpretation

This is messy, but meaningful:

👉 Structural vegetation + landscape vs active growth

Positive PC2 (top):

high vegetation cover
higher impervious influence
more canopy

Negative PC2 (bottom):

higher growth rate (slope)

👉 This is subtle but important:

You’re separating:

“already vegetated” ponds
vs
“actively changing” ponds

That’s a real ecological distinction.

PC3 (you cannot ignore this)
Key loadings:
canopy_50 → +0.58
imp_100 → -0.62

👉 This is your pure landscape gradient

PC3 = canopy vs impervious

This is exactly why stopping at PC2 is a mistake.

